<chapter xml:id="ch-statistical-modeling">
  <title>Statistical Modeling Layer</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The framework developed so far is descriptive. The baseline and advanced
      features of <xref ref="ch-baseline-features"/> and
      <xref ref="ch-group-features"/> give each
      <xref ref="gt-attempt" text="custom">attempt</xref> a stable feature
      vocabulary. The processing pipeline of <xref ref="ch-data-processing"/>
      produces analysis-ready tables with explicit quality-control flags. The
      displays of <xref ref="ch-visualization-diagnostics"/> make item-level
      patterns visible. This chapter adds the statistical modeling layer: the
      tools used to ask whether those patterns are stable, predictive, and
      interpretable after accounting for the structure of the data.
    </p>

    <p>
      The chapter first explains why descriptive summaries are not enough on
      their own. It then fixes the modeling units and the nested, crossed data
      structure; introduces regression models with item and student effects;
      treats process features as predictors; develops a simple distance-chain
      process model; describes trajectory clustering; and closes with validation
      across students, items, source versions, and semesters.
    </p>

    <p>
      The models here extend the descriptive framework; they do not replace it.
      A model should use the defined feature vocabulary, respect reconstruction
      flags, include item-design covariates when relevant, and be checked
      against held-out students, items, or semesters whenever the claim involves
      generalization.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-why-descriptive-features-are-not-enough">
    <title>Why Descriptive Features Are Not Enough</title>

    <p>
      Descriptive features answer questions such as: What fraction of students
      solved the item? How far was the first checked state from the
      <xref ref="gt-key" text="custom">key</xref>? Which
      <xref ref="gt-card" text="custom">cards</xref> were most often misplaced?
      How often were <xref ref="gt-decoy-card" text="custom">decoy cards</xref>
      or <xref ref="gt-decoy-slot" text="custom">decoy slots</xref> used? How
      many <xref ref="gt-move" text="custom">moves</xref>,
      <xref ref="gt-check" text="custom">checks</xref>,
      <xref ref="gt-reset" text="custom">resets</xref>, or
      <xref ref="gt-takeback" text="custom">takebacks</xref> occurred? Such
      summaries are necessary. They make the data legible, and the diagnostic
      layer of <xref ref="ch-visualization-diagnostics"/> is built from them.
    </p>

    <p>
      They are not sufficient because a difference in a descriptive feature does
      not identify its source. Suppose one item has a lower first-check correct
      rate than another. The difference may reflect greater conceptual
      difficulty, but it may also reflect more cards, more
      <xref ref="gt-slot" text="custom">slots</xref>, more decoys, a different
      feedback policy, a different cohort, or different prior instruction;
      <xref ref="subsec-design-versus-performance" text="title"/> made this
      point at the design level. A descriptive comparison alone cannot separate
      these possibilities.
    </p>

    <p>
      Statistical models let the framework ask separation questions:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Do process features predict eventual correctness after controlling
            for item and student differences?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Are <xref ref="gt-decoy-card-leak" text="custom">decoy-card
            leaks</xref> associated with lower final success across items, or
            only within particular items?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Do students who check frequently improve more after
            <xref ref="gt-feedback" text="custom">feedback</xref>, given the
            feedback policy under which they worked?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Are trajectory patterns stable across semesters?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Can item-level diagnostics predict which questions will produce many
            errors or resets in a future cohort?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Modeling does not remove the need for interpretation. A statistically
      significant coefficient is still a coefficient on a feature produced by a
      particular <xref ref="gt-item-design" text="custom">item design</xref>,
      reconstruction pipeline, and feedback environment. The model can support a
      claim only when the feature definition, processing choices, validation
      strategy, and interpretation all support it. That chain of custody is the
      subject of <xref ref="ch-validity-interpretation"/>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-nested-data-structure">
    <title>Nested Data Structure</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        A model is fit to a table, and the table commits the analysis to a unit:
        one row per what? This section fixes the available modeling levels, the
        nesting and crossing among them, and the table fields needed for
        reproducible modeling.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-modeling-levels">
      <title>Levels of Analysis</title>

      <p>
        The feature levels of <xref ref="tab-feature-levels"/> already run from
        single states up to cohorts. Modeling uses the same ladder. A modeling
        table may be built at any of the following levels:
        <dl width="medium">
          <li>
            <title>event level</title>
            <p>
              One row per logged event: a move, check, reset, or
              <xref ref="gt-page-view" text="custom">page view</xref>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>state level</title>
            <p>
              One row per reconstructed state or checked state.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>segment level</title>
            <p>
              One row per check segment, reset-free segment,
              <xref ref="gt-session" text="custom">session</xref>, or
              active-work segment.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>attempt level</title>
            <p>
              One row per student-item attempt under a stated attempt-boundary
              rule (<xref ref="subsec-attempt-boundary-options" text="title"/>).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>student level</title>
            <p>
              One row per student, aggregating behavior across items.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>item level</title>
            <p>
              One row per item version, aggregating behavior across students.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>course or semester level</title>
            <p>
              One row per offering, cohort, or instructional context.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        The unit determines the meaning of the outcome and predictors. A
        check-level model might predict whether a checked state is correct; an
        attempt-level model, whether the attempt eventually succeeds; an
        item-level model, the first-check correct rate of an item in a future
        semester. Every claim made from a model inherits the unit of the table on
        which the model was fit.
      </p>
    </subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-nesting-and-crossing">
  <title>Nesting and Crossing</title>

  <p>
    The levels are nested and crossed at the same time. Events sit inside
    attempts, and attempts belong to student-item pairs inside an offering:
    <md xml:id="an-data-nesting">
        \text{event}
      \subset
        \text{attempt}
      \subset
      (\text{student},\text{item})
      \subset
      \text{course}.
    </md>
    Students and items, however, are crossed: each student encounters many
    items, and each item is encountered by many students. The same item may
    also recur across semesters, sometimes with a changed source version or
    feedback policy.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because of this crossing, an attempt-level model usually needs both a
    student effect and an item effect. The student effect absorbs general
    differences among students; the item effect absorbs general differences
    among items. Omitting either risks a familiar confounding: attributing
    item difficulty to the students who happened to see the item, or student
    differences to the items they happened to attempt.
  </p>
</subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-modeling-table">
      <title>The Modeling Table</title>

      <p>
        A modeling table should be derived from the analysis-ready tables of
        <xref ref="tab-analysis-ready-tables"/>, not directly from raw logs. Each
        row should carry:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              identifiers for student, item, course, semester, and source
              version;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              the unit of analysis, such as attempt, check, segment, or event;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              the outcome variable or variables;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              the item-design covariates of
              <xref ref="tab-item-design-covariates"/>;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              baseline and advanced features, using the feature dictionary names
              (<xref ref="tab-minimal-baseline-feature-dictionary"/>);
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              the reconstruction flags of <xref ref="tab-quality-control-flags"/>
              and the readiness status (<c>ready</c>, <c>limited</c>, or
              <c>exclude</c>) of <xref ref="subsec-feature-readiness" text="title"/>;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              inclusion or exclusion status for the analysis being reported.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Flags and readiness status should be retained even when the main model
        excludes flagged rows. Keeping them makes it possible to report how much
        data were excluded and why, and to rerun the model under different
        inclusion rules. A conclusion that survives only one inclusion rule is a
        conclusion about that rule as much as about the students or items.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-item-and-student-effects">
    <title>Item and Student Effects</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Response features are produced by students working on particular items.
        Regression models with student and item effects are the basic tool for
        studying an outcome while respecting that structure.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-binary-outcome-model">
      <title>Binary Outcome Models</title>

      <p>
        Let <m>Y_{\nu q}</m> be a binary outcome for student <m>\nu</m> on item
        <m>q</m>, such as <c>first_check_correct</c> or
        <c>eventual_correct</c> from the check-outcome features of
        <xref ref="tab-check-outcome-features"/>.
      </p>

      <p>
        A basic mixed-effects logistic model is
        <md xml:id="an-binary-mixed-model">
          \operatorname{logit}\Pr(Y_{\nu q}=1)
          =
          \beta_0
          +
          \beta^{\top}\mathbf{z}_q
          +
          \gamma^{\top}\mathbf{x}_{\nu q}
          +
          u_\nu
          +
          v_q,
        </md>
        where <m>\mathbf{z}_q</m> collects item-design covariates,
        <m>\mathbf{x}_{\nu q}</m> collects attempt-level process features,
        <m>u_\nu</m> is a student effect, and <m>v_q</m> is an item effect. The
        boldface on <m>\mathbf{z}_q</m> and <m>\mathbf{x}_{\nu q}</m> emphasizes
        that these are covariate vectors; they are not zones or states.
      </p>

      <p>
        The design covariates <m>\mathbf{z}_q</m> come from
        <xref ref="tab-item-design-covariates"/>: <c>num_cards</c>,
        <c>num_slots</c>, <c>num_decoy_cards</c>, <c>num_decoy_slots</c>, and
        the feedback policy. The source version can join them as a covariate,
        but it is recorded as item-map provenance
        (<xref ref="subsec-source-versioning" text="title"/>), not in the
        covariate table. The process features
        <m>\mathbf{x}_{\nu q}</m> come from the feature dictionary: for example
        <c>first_check_distance</c>, <c>num_moves</c> before the first check,
        <c>num_checks</c>, a reset indicator, and decoy-leak counts such as
        <c>decoy_card_leak</c> and <c>decoy_slot_leak</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The effects <m>u_\nu</m> and <m>v_q</m> may be modeled as random effects,
        fixed effects, or regularized effects, depending on the size of the data
        and the intended claim. The notation above records the adjustment; the
        modeling choice must be reported with the results.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-continuous-or-count-outcomes">
      <title>Continuous and Count Outcomes</title>

      <p>
        Not every outcome is binary. Useful continuous, ordinal, or count-valued
        outcomes include:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <c>first_check_distance</c>, the key distance
              <m>d^\star(X_{k_1})</m> at the first check;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <c>corrected_progress</c>, the baseline-corrected progress score
              <m>g(X)</m> at a chosen check;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <c>num_moves</c>, the number of state-changing moves in the
              attempt or before the first check;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <c>num_checks</c> before success;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <c>active_time</c> on task;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              reset and takeback counts.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        Wherever a move count enters a model, it should count state-changing
        moves unless the analysis explicitly says otherwise. Rows flagged
        <c>noop_move</c> are excluded from <c>num_moves</c> by the reconstruction
        default of <xref ref="subsec-reconstruction-rules" text="title"/>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The model family should match the outcome scale. A key distance is a
        bounded count between <m>0</m> and <m>P</m>; a move count is a
        nonnegative count and may be overdispersed; active time is positive,
        skewed, and dependent on the idle-threshold rule that produced it. The
        construction of the outcome, not the convenience of the software, should
        drive the model choice.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-random-effects-interpretation">
      <title>Interpreting Effects</title>

      <p>
        Student effects summarize stable differences among students after
        accounting for the modeled features. Item effects summarize stable
        differences among items after accounting for the modeled design
        covariates. Neither should be overinterpreted. A large item effect may
        reflect conceptual difficulty, but it may also reflect wording, layout,
        feedback, source version, or unmeasured instructional context.
      </p>

      <p>
        Fixed-effect coefficients are conditional associations. A positive
        coefficient on <c>num_checks</c> does not mean that checking causes
        success: students who are close to the answer may simply keep checking,
        or the feedback policy may make checking strategically useful. As
        throughout the framework, the feedback policy <m>\phi</m> is part of the
        environment that produced the data
        (<xref ref="subsec-feedback-dependent-features" text="title"/>), and
        coefficients on feedback-sensitive features must be read relative to it.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-process-features-as-predictors">
    <title>Process Features as Predictors</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Process features summarize how a student interacted with the item before
        or between checked states. They are useful predictors because they carry
        information that the final answer alone does not.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-process-feature-families">
      <title>Feature Families</title>

      <p>
        The trajectory, check, and time features of
        <xref ref="ch-baseline-features"/> organize into predictor families:
        <dl width="medium">
          <li>
            <title>early-state features</title>
            <p>
              <c>first_check_distance</c>, the first-check error profile
              <m>\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}(X_{k_1})</m>, first-check decoy leaks,
              and first-check <xref ref="gt-occupancy" text="custom">occupancy</xref>
              residuals;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>movement features</title>
            <p>
              <c>num_moves</c>, fixing, lateral, and breaking counts,
              <c>excess_moves</c>, takebacks, and repeated states, computed
              within reset-free segments and excluding <c>noop_move</c> rows by
              default;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>feedback-use features</title>
            <p>
              check <c>cadence</c>, <c>post_check_gain</c>, moves after incorrect
              checks, and <c>responsiveness</c> <m>\rho_i</m> when the feedback
              policy highlights cards;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>timing features</title>
            <p>
              <c>time_to_first_move</c>, <c>time_to_first_check</c>,
              <c>median_move_gap</c>, <c>active_time</c>, and long idle gaps;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>reset features</title>
            <p>
              reset count, reset timing, and post-reset improvement.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        Grouping features from <xref ref="ch-group-features"/>
        join these families when grouping structure is part of the research
        question. The labeling gap <m>\widetilde{\sigma}-\sigma</m>
        distinguishes naming errors from grouping failures, and merge and split
        counts locate structural grouping errors at the first check.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-prediction-timing">
      <title>Temporal Alignment</title>

      <p>
        Predictors must occur before the outcome they predict. First-check
        features may predict eventual correctness; final-check features may not,
        because they already contain the outcome. The same discipline applies
        within an attempt. To model improvement after feedback, use only the
        state and feedback available at the earlier check as predictors. For
        example, features of <m>X_{k_i}</m> and the feedback returned at check
        <m>i</m> may be used to predict the later post-check gain
        <md>
          G_i=d^\star(X_{k_i})-d^\star(X_{k_{i+1}}),
        </md>
        as defined in <xref ref="an-baseline-post-check-gain" text="custom">the
        baseline chapter</xref>. The gain itself is the outcome; it is not known
        until the next checked state exists.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-predictive-versus-explanatory">
      <title>Predictive and Explanatory Uses</title>

      <p>
        Process features serve two different goals. A predictive model asks
        whether the features improve prediction for new students, items, or
        semesters; it is judged on held-out data. An explanatory model asks
        whether a feature is associated with an outcome after accounting for
        other variables; it is judged by the justification of its covariates, the
        temporal ordering of its predictors, and the care of its interpretation.
      </p>

      <p>
        The two standards do not substitute for each other. A feature that
        improves prediction is not thereby a causal explanation, and a carefully
        argued association may still fail to predict well for the next cohort.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-process-models">
    <title>Process Models</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The regressions above treat process features as inputs. A process model
        treats the trajectory itself as the object being modeled. This section
        develops a simple process model for drag-and-drop data: a Markov chain on
        distance to the key. Its fundamental matrix turns pooled transitions into
        an expected number of moves to solve.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-markov">
      <title>Markov Chain</title>

      <p>
        The full <xref ref="gt-configuration-space" text="custom">configuration
        space</xref> has <m>(R+1)^P</m> states, usually too many to model from
        item-level data. Coarsening each state to its key distance, as in the
        distance curve <m>d_k=d^\star(X_k)</m> of
        <xref ref="an-baseline-distance-curve" text="custom">the baseline
        chapter</xref>, collapses the trajectory onto the finite distance space
        <md>
          d\in\{0,1,\dots,P\}.
        </md>
        The move classification of <xref ref="subsec-move-classification"
        text="title"/> then describes the local transition structure: a
        <term>fix</term> steps <m>d</m> down by one, a <term>lateral</term>
        leaves it unchanged, a <term>break</term> steps it up by one, and a reset
        (<xref ref="an-reset-state-update" text="custom">the trajectory
        model</xref>) returns the board to the starting state <m>X_0</m> and
        hence to distance <m>d^\star(X_0)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pooling a cohort's retained board-changing actions estimates the
        one-step transition probabilities
        <md xml:id="an-distance-transition-probability">
          p(d\to d')
          =
          \Pr(d_{k+1}=d'\mid d_k=d),
        </md>
        where each probability is estimated as the fraction of observed
        transitions out of a distance-<m>d</m> board that land at distance
        <m>d'</m>. <xref ref="fig-cohort-markov"/> shows this estimation step
        before any chain is solved: the mix of fixes, laterals, breaks, and
        reset jumps out of each starting distance.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cohort-markov">
        <caption>
          The distance chain as an action mix: for each starting distance, the
          share of transitions that <term>fix</term> (decrease the distance by
          one), stay <term>lateral</term>, <term>break</term> (increase the
          distance by one), or <term>jump</term> by reset. The mix is read from
          the data before the chain is solved; a high break or reset share is a
          process-level signal of thrashing or starting over.
        </caption>
        <image source="figures/dnd-cohort-markov.png" width="60%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Stacked bar chart, one bar per starting distance, partitioned into
              the fix, lateral, break, and reset-jump shares of transitions taken
              from that distance.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Treating the next board action as depending only on the current distance
        makes the distance sequence a Markov chain with the
        <xref ref="gt-solved" text="custom">solved</xref> state <m>d=0</m>
        absorbing. Let <m>Q</m> be the transient block of the estimated
        transition matrix: the rows and columns corresponding to distances
        <m>d&gt;0</m>. If absorption is reachable from each retained transient
        distance, then the fundamental matrix gives the expected number of moves
        to absorption from each transient distance:
        <md xml:id="an-expected-moves-to-solve">
          \mathbf{t}
          =
          (I-Q)^{-1}\mathbf{1}.
        </md>
        The entry of <m>\mathbf{t}</m> at the starting distance
        <m>d^\star(X_0)</m> is the model's <term>expected moves to solve</term>,
        a process-level difficulty measure. <xref ref="fig-cohort-markov-matrix"/>
        shows the estimated transition matrix used in this computation.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cohort-markov-matrix">
        <caption>
          The estimated transition matrix <m>p(d \to d')</m>, including
          absorption at the solved state <m>d=0</m>. Each row is a starting
          distance and each column is the next distance. The entries leading into
          <m>d=0</m> determine how quickly the chain can reach the key.
        </caption>
        <image source="figures/dnd-cohort-markov_matrix.png" width="55%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Heatmap of the estimated one-step transition probability from each
              distance to each other distance.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The approximation has two layers, both of which should be reported:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              the coarsening forgets <em>which</em> cards are wrong, so states
              with the same distance but different repair paths are pooled; and
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              the memoryless assumption is too strong for a feedback-guided
              student, whose next move may depend on the last check, the last
              feedback, and earlier failed placements.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For the four-shape question, the starting distance is
            <m>d^\star(X_0)=3</m>. For a compact illustrative calculation, cap
            the reported distance states at <m>3</m>, so any excursion to a
            larger distance is folded into the <m>d=3</m> row. With illustrative
            one-step probabilities estimated from pooled board actions, the
            transition matrix over <m>\{0,1,2,3\}</m> is shown in
            <xref ref="tab-cheat-markov"/>.
          </p>

          <table xml:id="tab-cheat-markov">
            <title>Illustrative one-step transition probabilities; row <m>d=0</m> is absorbing, and each row sums to <m>1</m>.</title>
            <tabular halign="center" bottom="minor">
              <col width="24%"/>
              <col width="19%"/>
              <col width="19%"/>
              <col width="19%"/>
              <col width="19%"/>
              <row bottom="medium">
                <cell><p>from / to</p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>d=0</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>d=1</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>d=2</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>d=3</m></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><p><m>d=0</m> (solved)</p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>1</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0</m></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><p><m>d=1</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0.7</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0.1</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0.1</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0.1</m></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell><p><m>d=2</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0.6</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0.2</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0.2</m></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><p><m>d=3</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0.6</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>0.4</m></p></cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
          </table>

          <p>
            Only <m>d=1</m> carries absorption mass in this example, since a
            single fixing move can reach the key only from one card out of
            place. Dropping the absorbing row and column leaves the transient
            block <m>Q</m> and its complement:
            <md>
              Q=\begin{bmatrix}
                0.1 \amp 0.1 \amp 0.1 \\
                0.6 \amp 0.2 \amp 0.2 \\
                0   \amp 0.6 \amp 0.4
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              I-Q=\begin{bmatrix}
                0.9  \amp -0.1 \amp -0.1 \\
                -0.6 \amp 0.8  \amp -0.2 \\
                0    \amp -0.6 \amp 0.6
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </md>
          </p>

          <p>
            The expected number of moves to solve from each distance is
            <m>\mathbf{t}=(I-Q)^{-1}\mathbf{1}</m>. Equivalently, with
            <m>t_0=0</m>, the one-step recursions are
            <md>
              <mrow>t_1 \amp= 1 + 0.1\,t_1 + 0.1\,t_2 + 0.1\,t_3,</mrow>
              <mrow>t_2 \amp= 1 + 0.6\,t_1 + 0.2\,t_2 + 0.2\,t_3,</mrow>
              <mrow>t_3 \amp= 1 + 0.6\,t_2 + 0.4\,t_3.</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>

          <p>
            Solving from the bottom up, the <m>d=3</m> equation gives
            <m>t_3=t_2+\tfrac{5}{3}</m>; substituting into the <m>d=2</m>
            equation gives <m>t_2=t_1+\tfrac{20}{9}</m>; and the <m>d=1</m>
            equation gives <m>0.7\,t_1=\tfrac{29}{18}</m>. Hence
            <md>
              t_1=\tfrac{145}{63}\approx2.30,\qquad
              t_2=\tfrac{95}{21}\approx4.52,\qquad
              t_3=\tfrac{130}{21}\approx6.19.
            </md>
            The headline value is <m>t_3</m>: from the empty board, this
            illustrative cohort is expected to take about <m>6.2</m> board
            actions to solve, compared with the theoretical floor
            <m>d^\star(X_0)=3</m>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fundamental matrix itself,
            <md>
              (I-Q)^{-1}=\begin{bmatrix}
                \tfrac{10}{7} \amp \tfrac{10}{21} \amp \tfrac{25}{63} \\
                \tfrac{10}{7} \amp \tfrac{15}{7}  \amp \tfrac{20}{21} \\
                \tfrac{10}{7} \amp \tfrac{15}{7}  \amp \tfrac{55}{21}
              \end{bmatrix},
            </md>
            has an entrywise interpretation: its <m>(i,j)</m> entry is the
            expected number of visits to transient distance <m>j</m> before
            solving, starting from transient distance <m>i</m>. The row sums are
            the entries of <m>\mathbf{t}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <assemblage xml:id="asm-markov-why">
        <title>Why the approximation is useful</title>
        <p>
          The Markov property is only an approximation, but the distance chain
          earns its place for five reasons.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <em>Comparable process summaries.</em> It compresses a cohort of
                irregular trajectories into a small set of quantities, especially
                expected moves to solve and excess over the theoretical floor.
                These summaries can be compared across items when reported with
                <m>P</m>, <m>R</m>, and the starting distance.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <em>Interpretable transition rates.</em> The fitted rates are
                diagnostics even before the matrix is inverted: a high break rate
                suggests instability, a high reset rate suggests starting over,
                and a high fix rate suggests steady convergence.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <em>A baseline for history effects.</em> The predicted path
                length gives a reference value. Large gaps between observed move
                counts and model-based expectations identify attempts or items
                where history, feedback, or strategy may matter.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <em>Low data demand.</em> The model is estimated from pooled
                transition counts and needs far less data than the full
                <m>(R+1)^P</m> configuration graph
                (<xref ref="an-configuration-graph" text="custom">the trajectory
                model</xref>).
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <em>Clear refinement path.</em> Because the approximation is
                explicit, it can be tested and refined. With enough data, the
                macro-state can include recent feedback, the per-slot-correct
                signature, or the error profile
                <m>\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}(X)</m>
                (<xref ref="an-baseline-error-profile" text="custom">the
                baseline chapter</xref>) instead of distance alone.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <remark xml:id="rmk-markov-floor">
        <title>The starting distance is not always the maximum distance</title>
        <p>
          For the running example, <m>d^\star(X_0)=3</m>, because the three
          non-decoy cards begin in the tray and the decoy card is already in its
          home zone. A student can nevertheless move the decoy card out of the
          tray and reach distance <m>4</m>. Thus the mathematical distance range
          is <m>\{0,1,\dots,P\}</m>, not merely
          <m>\{0,1,\dots,d^\star(X_0)\}</m>. An analyst may include the extra
          distance state, cap larger distances at the starting distance, or use a
          different coarsening, but the choice is part of the model definition and
          should be reported.
        </p>
      </remark>

      <p>
        Like every summary in this framework, expected moves to solve describes
        movement under a feedback policy. It is not direct evidence of a
        reasoning process. It should be reported with the cohort, item version,
        feedback policy, distance coarsening, and readiness filter that produced
        the pooled transitions.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-trajectory-clustering">
    <title>Trajectory Clustering</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Some research questions concern patterns of interaction rather than a
        single outcome. Trajectory clustering groups attempts with similar
        process structure so that common response paths can be inspected
        together.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-clustering-representations">
      <title>Representing a Trajectory for Clustering</title>

      <p>
        A trajectory must be represented before it can be clustered. Common
        representations, from most compressed to least compressed, include:
        <dl width="medium">
          <li>
            <title>feature vector</title>
            <p>
              Each attempt becomes its process features: move counts, checks,
              resets, gains, error profiles, and timing summaries.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>distance curve</title>
            <p>
              Each attempt becomes its sequence <m>d_0,d_1,\dots,d_n</m>,
              aligned or resampled to a common length.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>event string</title>
            <p>
              Each attempt becomes a string over event types, such as fixing
              move, lateral move, breaking move, takeback, check, and reset.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>state sequence</title>
            <p>
              Each attempt keeps its full <xref ref="gt-configuration" text="custom">configuration</xref>
              sequence <m>X_0,X_1,\dots,X_n</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        Different representations answer different questions. Feature-vector
        clustering groups attempts with similar summaries. Distance-curve
        clustering groups attempts with similar progress shapes. State-sequence
        clustering groups attempts that visit similar configurations.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-trajectory-distance-functions">
      <title>Distances Between Trajectories</title>

      <p>
        Clustering requires a distance between attempts. Possible choices include
        Euclidean distance between standardized feature vectors, distance between
        aligned progress curves, edit distance between
        <xref ref="gt-event" text="custom">event</xref> strings, dynamic alignment
        distances for trajectories of different lengths, and graph-based
        distances between visited-state sets or transition-count vectors. When
        grouping structure is the object of interest, the variation of
        information of <xref ref="an-variation-of-information" text="custom">the
        advanced comparison chapter</xref> supplies a metric between partition
        structures of checked states.
      </p>

      <p>
        The distance function determines the clusters. Two attempts may be close
        under one representation and far apart under another, so the
        representation and distance should be chosen to match the research
        question, not to produce visually appealing clusters.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-cluster-interpretation">
      <title>Interpreting Clusters</title>

      <p>
        Clusters are descriptive groupings, not direct evidence of cognitive
        strategies. A cluster with many checks might reflect guessing, strategic
        feedback use, or careful incremental revision, depending on the item and
        its feedback policy. A cluster with few moves might reflect confidence;
        if its checked states remain far from the key, it may instead reflect
        disengagement or an early stop.
      </p>

      <p>
        Interpretation should return to representative attempts, item design,
        checked states, feedback, and timing. A useful cluster report includes
        the number of attempts per cluster, representative trajectories, outcome
        summaries by cluster, item and student composition by cluster, and
        reconstruction-quality summaries by cluster. The last is essential: a
        cluster driven by <c>check_mismatch</c> or parsing flags is a data-quality
        finding, not a behavioral one.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-cluster-stability">
      <title>Cluster Stability</title>

      <p>
        Clustering is sensitive to preprocessing, feature scaling, the distance
        function, and the number of clusters. A cluster solution should be
        checked for stability by varying these choices and asking whether the
        same broad patterns survive.
      </p>

      <p>
        If clusters feed later modeling or interpretation, they should also be
        validated on held-out data or across semesters. A pattern that appears in
        one cohort and vanishes in the next is exploratory until the difference
        is explained.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-cross-semester-validation">
    <title>Cross-Semester Validation</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        A framework intended for research should produce features, models, and
        patterns that are not artifacts of a single course offering.
        Cross-semester validation asks whether item-level, process-level, and
        model-level findings reappear when the framework is applied to new
        cohorts.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-validation-splits">
      <title>Validation Splits</title>

      <p>
        Useful splits mirror the nesting structure of
        <xref ref="an-data-nesting" text="custom">the modeling table</xref>:
        <dl width="medium">
          <li>
            <title>student holdout</title>
            <p>
              Fit on some students and evaluate on held-out students from the
              same items and semester.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>item holdout</title>
            <p>
              Fit on some items and evaluate on held-out items. This asks
              whether design covariates and process features generalize to new
              questions.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>semester holdout</title>
            <p>
              Fit on one offering and evaluate on a later offering.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>source-version holdout</title>
            <p>
              Fit on one version of an item and evaluate after the source or
              feedback policy changes.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        The split should match the claim. A model that survives student holdout
        has not thereby generalized to new items or semesters, and item-level
        claims require item-level validation.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-stability-of-features">
      <title>Stability of Features</title>

      <p>
        Before validating a model, check whether the descriptive features
        themselves are stable: first-check correct rate, mean first-check
        distance, decoy-card leak rate, decoy-slot usage, mean checks, reset
        rate, and reconstruction-quality rates, each by semester. These are the
        same cross-cohort comparisons the diagnostic layer already produces
        (<xref ref="subsec-cross-course-and-semester-stability" text="title"/>),
        now read as a precondition for modeling.
      </p>

      <p>
        Feature instability is not automatically a problem. It may reflect real
        instructional differences, item wording changes, feedback-policy
        changes, or cohort differences. But it must be documented before the
        features are treated as stable item characteristics in a model.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-model-performance">
      <title>Model Performance</title>

      <p>
        Predictive models should be evaluated with metrics that match the
        outcome: calibration and discrimination measures for binary outcomes,
        prediction error and residual structure for continuous or count outcomes,
        in each case computed on the held-out split.
      </p>

      <p>
        Performance should also be reported separately for relevant strata:
        semester, item type, feedback policy, and readiness status. A model that
        performs well overall may still fail on items with many decoys, traces
        with long idle gaps, or the <c>limited</c> stratum of the readiness
        classification. Averaging over those failures hides exactly the cases a
        diagnostic framework is meant to catch.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-model-transportability">
      <title>Transportability and Limits</title>

      <p>
        Cross-semester validation separates patterns local to one dataset from
        patterns that may transport to future offerings. Transportable patterns
        are valuable for item diagnostics because they suggest the feature
        reflects something stable about the item or interaction design rather
        than the particular students who happened to work it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Even a model that validates across semesters remains bounded by the data
        that built it. A model trained on one course, platform version, feedback
        policy, or family of drag-and-drop items should not be assumed to apply
        unchanged elsewhere.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reporting-models">
      <title>Reporting Modeling Results</title>

      <p>
        A modeling report should state the unit of analysis; the outcome
        definition; the predictors and their timing relative to the outcome; the
        item-design covariates; the student, item, course, or semester effects
        included; the exclusion rules and readiness summaries; the validation
        split and performance metrics; and the limits on interpretation. Without
        these details, a coefficient or prediction score cannot be interpreted,
        reproduced, or compared across studies.
      </p>

      <p>
        The limits on interpretation are larger than a checklist item. A
        validated model establishes that a pattern is stable and predictive;
        whether the pattern supports a claim about student reasoning,
        misconceptions, feedback use, or learning is a separate argument. The
        final chapter, <xref ref="ch-validity-interpretation"/>, takes up that
        argument by distinguishing what the framework measures directly from
        what must be inferred.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
